🚩Resurrect the Temple of Democracy

@The editor of New  Indian express writes that the malady afflicting the world's most populous democracy is not that its politicians talk too much, but that they have forgotten how to talk to one another.The cold numbers of Parliament proceedings since 2024 tell the story more soberly than the overheated wrhetoric. 

@According to PRS Legislative Research, the Lok Sabha functioned for a dismal 15 percent of its scheduled time in the Monsoon Session of 2026. It was perhaps the worst performance since the demonetisation winter of 2016. Question Hour, that ritual of accountability survived for a pitiful nine minutes, and nine of the 11 Bills the lower House passed sailed through without a single member other than the minister uttering a word. 

@The previous Monsoon Session had forfeited more than 84 hours to forced adjournments, and the Winter Session of 2024 limped along at 52 percent in the Lok Sabha and a meagre 39 percent in the Rajya Sabha. The pattern is shocking. Whenever politics turns poisonous, Parliament becomes its first and most defenceless casualty The Treasury benches accuse the opposition of disruption and fili bustering. The opposition replies with a bitter litany of rejected notices, muted microphones and the Leader of the Opposition denied the floor. Even urgent demands for discussing the electoral rolls, General Naravane's unpublished memoir and the NEET leak are brushed aside by a brute majority that prioritises the passage of Bills over arguments.

@When opponents are recast asenemies, every compromise is condemned as capitulation and every handshake suspected as betrayal.

@The bargain on which parliamentary democracy rests is elegantly simple: the opposition must have its say and the government must have its way. Take away the first and majority rule curdles into majoritarian rule; strip away the second and principled argument hardens into sterile obstruction. Lose that balance and politics degenerates into a permanent campaign in which governance is the unpaid casualty, while unemployment, inflation and agrarian distress wait forlornly in the ante-room as non-issues.

@The government must make gener ous room for the opposition's agenda, admit adjournment motions, elect a long-overdue Deputy Speaker and treat the Leader of the Opposition as a constitutional office rather than a partisan nuisance. The opposition must accept that a House it paralyses can hold no one to account and must seize offers of debate rather than dismiss them as stagecraft.India's leaders have mastered the confrontation; they must now relearn the conversation.

